Short oval does something slightly different. It follows the natural curve of the nail bed itself, which reads as soft and rounded rather than architectural. Where squoval works because it borrows structure, short oval works because it borrows nothing, it simply follows what is already there. Choose squoval for a cleaner, more graphic fall look. Choose short oval when the shade is doing the talking and the shape should stay quiet.

Almond and coffin remain the wrong call here, not because they are bad shapes, but because they are shape decisions that only pay off with length. A tech forcing an almond taper onto a short bed usually ends up filing away more nail than necessary just to fake the point, which is the opposite of what a fall manicure on short nails should be doing. If almond genuinely interests you as a longer-term goal, <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/short-almond-nails'>the complete guide to short almond nails and why the shape works at any length</a> is worth reading before committing a fall set to it.

There's a real data point behind why short is having a moment beyond preference: nail trend tracking has shown a sharp rise in demand for short, classic manicures over the last several seasons, a shift toward wearability over drama that fall's deeper colour palette actually suits well. Short and intentional was never a placeholder. It became its own category.

