## French Tips, Reimagined for Short Fall Nails

Proportion, not length, is the actual rule with a French tip on a short nail. <a href='/blog/fall/fall-french-tip-nails'>Fall french tip nails</a> in the classic wide white-tip format were designed with more free edge to work across, and applying that same width to a shorter nail throws off the ratio between base and tip.

The fix is a <strong>micro French tip</strong>, a thin-lined French tip of one to two millimetres, proportioned specifically for <strong>short fall nails</strong> instead of scaled down from a longer-nail design. Swap the classic white line for chocolate brown, rust, or a soft metallic and the tip reads as fall-specific rather than a leftover summer style. A hairline tip in a warm bronze chrome, for instance, catches light without needing any length to show it off.

Getting that proportion right is a technical detail worth taking seriously, since a French tip that's even slightly too wide on a short nail can visually shorten it further rather than lengthen it. <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/french-nails/short-french-tip-nails'>How to proportion a French tip correctly on short nails</a> goes further into the exact ratio to request at the salon or replicate at home.

