## Low-Maintenance Short Fall Nails for Busy Weeks

A single confident shade does more for <strong>short fall nails</strong> than a design ever needs to. The instinct on a busy week is to assume low-maintenance means boring, but a single-shade manicure in a genuinely good fall colour, matte cinnamon, warm clay, deep olive, reads as a deliberate choice rather than a placeholder.

This is the version to reach for between salon visits or during a stretch with no time for anything more involved. One coat of a strengthening base, two coats of colour, and a matte top coat. No gradient to blend, no accent nail to plan around, no drying time for layered art. It holds through a full week of glove season without a single chip showing more than a simple base-and-colour set would anyway.

Low-maintenance and low-effort aren't the same thing here. The effort goes into picking the right shade and finish once, and the payoff is a set that looks intentional for the two to three weeks it takes to grow out, not a set that needs constant upkeep to keep looking chosen rather than default.

